<h2>China's scale in the Indian shrimp trade</h2>
<p>China took 136,164 MT of Indian frozen shrimp in FY2024-25 — the second-largest single-country market after the United States, and well ahead of the European Union's 99,310 MT. This isn't a niche or emerging relationship; it's one of the two anchor markets that the Indian shrimp export industry is built around.</p>

<h2>GACC registration comes before anything else</h2>
<p>If there's one thing to sort out before you commit to a supplier or a timeline, it's this. China's Decree 248 requires facility-level registration with the General Administration of Customs of China (GACC) before many food categories, seafood included, can clear Chinese customs. This registration applies to the specific processing facility, and it's a process with real lead time — not something that can be resolved during your first shipment's transit.</p>
<p>If you're working with us for the first time on China-bound shrimp, confirm registration status at the enquiry stage. It's a simple question with a potentially long answer if it hasn't been done yet.</p>

<h2>Don't assume another market's compliance transfers</h2>
<p>This trips up buyers who source from the same supplier for multiple destinations. China sets its own residue and quality standards independently — they aren't harmonised with EU or US limits, and product tested and cleared for one market isn't automatically compliant for China. Tell us specifically that China is your destination, and we'll test and document against the applicable Chinese standard rather than assuming a US or EU clearance carries over.</p>

<h2>Form and specification</h2>
<p>As with any shrimp order, specify form (HOSO, HLSO, PD, PUD, PDTO or cooked), count size, and whether you need IQF or block-frozen. Chinese buyers span the full range of use cases — some are importing for further domestic processing (favoring HOSO or HLSO), others for direct retail or food service (often PD or cooked). Tell us your intended use and we'll recommend the right form rather than defaulting to whatever's easiest to quote.</p>

<h2>Logistics and timing</h2>
<p>Transit runs 15-22 days to major Chinese ports (Shanghai, Shenzhen/Yantian, Qingdao) — mid-range compared with our other lanes, shorter than the US route and longer than Southeast Asia. Plan your registration and documentation lead time separately from shipping transit; the GACC process is usually the longer pole in the timeline for a first order.</p>

<h2>Working with an existing Chinese import relationship</h2>
<p>Many Chinese shrimp importers are established seafood trading companies with existing GACC registration relationships and customs clearance processes already in place. If you're working with one, the practical hurdles are lower — your main job is agreeing specification, form and pricing, since the regulatory pathway is already proven. If you're a newer entrant or importing for your own processing operation for the first time, budget more time for the registration and clearance learning curve, and lean on us for guidance based on other China-bound shipments we've handled.</p>
